+# Hunt report: export worker acceptance and restart recovery
+
+Target: `app/export_queue.py` (`Queue.submit`, `Queue.work`, `Queue.recover`)
+against `app/export-contract.md`. Scope was limited to this one operation:
+whether accepted jobs still produce their result after a process
+interruption, and the recovery path that makes that happen.
+
+## Result
+
+**I found no bugs in scope.** Every interruption point allowed by the contract
+leads to the result being produced after restart or retry.
+
+## Governing records
+
+None of the records in `specs/` (`ARCH-app`, `SPEC-note-payload`,
+`CLAIM-single-writer`, `GATE-local-only`, `REQ-import-source`) mention
+`export_queue.py` or the jobs and results database. The only governing source
+is `app/export-contract.md`. I did not change any records.
+
+## Contract assumptions used
+
+- Committed SQLite transactions survive process termination. Storage loss and
+  corruption are out of scope.
+- Only one worker runs at a time, and a restart begins only after the previous
+  worker has stopped. There are no concurrent requests.
+- After recovery, the application calls `work` repeatedly. Transient I/O
+  failures are retried.
+- A job ID always has the same payload, so a duplicate submit with a different
+  payload is out of scope.
+
+## Analysis
+
+| Interruption point | Durable state | Recovery path | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Before the `submit` commit | No row | Not accepted (the caller gets no `accepted` response) | No obligation |
+| After `submit` returns | `jobs` row with state `pending` | `work` selects pending jobs | Result produced |
+| Between the claim commit and the output (the `after_claim` hook) | State `running`, no result | `recover()` resets `running` to `pending` on restart. `work` also selects `running` jobs, which covers a retry in the same process | Result produced |
+| During the output transaction | Rolled back atomically to `running` | Same as the previous row | Result produced |
+| After the output commits | `results` row exists and the state is `done` | `done` jobs are not selected | No further work, as the contract requires |
+
+Other points checked:
+
+- The result insert and the `done` update happen in one transaction, so a
+  result can never exist without its job being marked `done`, and vice versa.
+  The plain `INSERT INTO results` therefore can't hit a duplicate key on retry.
+- `submit` uses `INSERT OR IGNORE`, so resubmitting a job ID never resets a
+  `done` job or replaces its payload. Under the contract that is correct.
+- The read in `work` happens outside a transaction. That is safe only because
+  there is a single worker and requests never overlap, which the contract
+  guarantees.
+
+## Executable witness
+
+`tests/hunt_witness.py` (run with `python3 tests/hunt_witness.py`) exits 0 if
+the obligation holds and 1 if it is violated. It covers:
+
+- Hard termination of a child process with `os._exit` at three points: after
+  `submit`, at the `after_claim` boundary, and after completion. Each is
+  followed by a restart in a fresh `Queue` and repeated `work` calls.
+- An exception raised at the hook followed by a retry in the same process.
+- Repeated interruptions across several restarts.
+
+On the current code, all checks pass (exit 0). As a sanity check, I ran the
+witness against a temporary copy of `app/` with the recovery logic removed:
+`recover()` made a no-op and `work` limited to `pending` jobs. Three checks
+failed and it exited 1, so the witness does detect a broken recovery path.
+That copy was deleted, and the product code was not modified.
+
+## Not covered
+
+- Behavior that is out of contract: concurrent workers, overlapping requests,
+  storage loss or corruption, and reusing a job ID with a different payload.
+- Other modules (`handler`, `store`, `exporter`, `config`, `metadata`), per the
+  requested stopping point.
